noted per epic.\n7. Effort: E005 + routing branch epic together carry the strategy's 2-3 sprint operator estimate; split along HLR-5/HLR-6 lines so each sits near the ~2-week guideline. E003 carries 1-2 sprints per strategy arithmetic (within guideline).\n\nTiered Delivery: Not applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; all dependencies are internal and exist.\n\nStaff Engineer Input traceability: parsed first, takes precedence; the strategy body as fetched already reflects all three corrections (no new flag — combine existing flags; split-server deployment not config toggle; no SSAR prescription — auth reuses existing plugin infra). No divergence between Staff input and the technical approach remained to override.\n\nOpen question dispositions table:\n| Open question | Disposition |\n| OQ1 latency | AC on E003 (measure; caching if threshold exceeded) — implementation proceeds the same way; caching is fix-and-retry per the strategy's own mitigation |\n| OQ2 routing | E002 Investigation + conditional branches |\n| OQ3 plugin init coupling | E001 Investigation (merged with assumptions A2/A5) |\n| OQ4 audit channel | scope item on E003, default server logs per HLR-8; PM/Security confirmation |\n\nComponent mapping section.\n\nConditional branches section: describe branch A and B.\n\nNow epic bodies. Let me write them carefully with the four required sections, no sibling epic references.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\n# Validate workspace resolution in the artifacts-only server context\n\n## Description\nResolves whether the existing workspace resolution machinery — the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin (kubernetes-auth, workspace provider registered via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`) and the `kubernetes://` workspace store — can resolve experiment-to-workspace membership in an MLflow server running in `--artifacts-only` mode, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The answer determines the scope of the plugin and server implementation work: verification only, or refactoring workspace resolution into an initialization path independent of tracking store availability.\n\n## Scope\n- Determine whether workspace store initialization in `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` depends on tracking store or model registry store availability, and map the plugin's initialization dependencies.\n- Determine whether the `kubernetes://` workspace store can be queried for experiment-to-workspace mapping without initializing tracking stores.\n- Validate that artifact request paths (scoped to the S3 artifact store layout `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`, the standard RHOAI configuration) carry sufficient information (experiment ID or run ID) to resolve workspace membership; document limitations of non-standard artifact roots.\n- Deliver a written assessment with a pass/fail verdict per question and, if coupling is found, a refactoring plan that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written assessment exists that states, with code-level references, whether workspace store initialization in the auth plugin depends on tracking store availability, and whether the workspace store is queryable without tracking stores.\n- A written verdict states whether artifact request paths in the S3 artifact store layout provide the information needed to resolve workspace membership, including documented edge cases for non-standard artifact roots.\n- If any coupling is found, the assessment includes a concrete refactoring plan for an independent workspace-resolution initialization path, sufficient to scope the follow-on implementation.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P0, support): establishes that workspace validation of artifact requests is feasible before the enforcement work starts.\n- HLR-7 (P1): resolves the open question of whether plugin workspace resolution functions without tracking store initialization.\n\nHmm — HLR traceability for an investigation: it doesn't \"cover\" HLRs, it de-risks them. I'll phrase as above. Also note assumptions A1/A2/A5? A1 is server init composability — actually I said A1 goes to E003. Let me include A2/A5 in E001.\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Design artifact traffic routing for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\nResolves how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to a dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology: purely through Gateway API HTTPRoute configuration, or via a tracking-server configuration change that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The answer selects between the two conditional routing implementation branches and determines whether any server-side configuration work exists.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluate routing options for directing artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, consistent with the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern and conditional Gateway API HTTPRoute creation via runtime CRD discovery.\n- Determine whether gateway-level routing alone can split artifact traffic while preserving workspace validation on the artifacts-only instance, or whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations.\n- Define the HTTPRoute design for the artifacts-only instance's ingress under the selected option (host/path shape).\n- Deliver a written design decision with the selected routing mechanism and its implications for the operator templates and, if applicable, the tracking server configuration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written design decision identifies the routing mechanism (gateway-level routing only, or routing requiring a tracking-server configuration change) with rationale grounded in the operator's existing routing patterns.\n- The design specifies the HTTPRoute resources needed for the artifacts-only instance and the configuration the main tracking deployment needs to route artifact traffic.\n- If server-side delegation is required, the design specifies the configuration surface on the tracking server that must change.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (P1): resolves how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance.\n- HLR-5 (P1, support): the selected design shapes the HTTPRoute created for the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\nMakes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores and model registry stores uninitialized with non-artifact API endpoints disabled. Each artifact request is resolved to its parent experiment, its workspace assignment looked up via the workspace store, and authorization delegated to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; failing requests get HTTP 403. Behavior is fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable, and audit logging records workspace access denials. No new server flag is introduced.\n\n## Scope\n- Combine `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact request validation.\n- Keep tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled in the combined mode.\n- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (S3 artifact store layout `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny with HTTP 403 on workspace membership failure.\n- Implement fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable) rather than serving without validation.\n- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation; no existing CLI arguments or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs (default channel per the strategy's HLR; confirm the telemetry channel with PM/Security — OTLP or Kubernetes audit log — before implementation and adjust the emission mechanism if a different channel is mandated).\n- Measure per-request workspace resolution latency; if it exceeds the threshold agreed with PM/Engineering, implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path (otherwise record caching as a follow-up per the PM decision).\n- Regression coverage: single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given the artifacts-only server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- Given `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`, authenticated users receive artifacts without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership).\n- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error.\n- Workspace access denials produce audit records through server logs (or the PM/Security-selected channel) containing enough context to identify the denied request.\n- A measured latency figure for per-request workspace resolution is documented, with the caching decision (implemented or deferred) recorded against the PM/Engineering-agreed threshold.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): flag composability — covered.\n- HLR-2 (P0): workspace validation of artifact requests with HTTP 403 denials — covered.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior — covered.\n- HLR-4 (P0): unchanged behavior for single-tenant / non-workspace deployments — covered.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging of workspace access denials is bundled here rather than split into its own epic — it instruments the same workspace-denial call sites as HLR-2/HLR-3, so a separate epic would revisit the same validation code paths; not separately schedulable.\n\nNote: \"the plugin's verified resolution\" — I shouldn't reference E004 by ID but can describe. In scope I wrote \"delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure\" — fine.\n\n**E004 body:**\n\n# Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\n\n## Description\nVerifies and, where needed, extends the `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` auth plugin so that workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace mapping via the workspace provider registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`) functions correctly in the artifacts-only server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` authentication setup are unchanged.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution against the artifacts-only server context, per the validated assessment of its initialization dependencies.\n- If workspace resolution is coupled to the full tracking server initialization path, refactor it into an independent initialization path within the plugin so the artifacts-only server can initialize only the workspace provider.\n- Ensure workspace resolution can be driven from artifact request paths (experiment/run identity) without tracking store availability.\n- Keep the plugin's behavior for the fully-initialized tracking server unchanged (no regression to non-artifact workspace operations).\n- Unit tests covering workspace resolution in both initialization contexts (full server and artifacts-only).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Workspace resolution returns the correct workspace assignment for an experiment in a context where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized, demonstrated by automated tests.\n- Existing workspace resolution behavior for the fully-initialized tracking server is unchanged (existing plugin tests pass).\n- If refactoring was required, the independent initialization path is landed with tests demonstrating the plugin initializes without tracking store availability.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-7 (P1): auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization.\n- HLR-2 (P0, support): provides the resolution the server's 403 enforcement builds on.\n\n**E005 body:**\n\n# Dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment in the MLflow operator\n\n## Description\nExtends the MLflow operator's Helm-in-operator pattern to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute, distinct from the main tracking Deployment, and to propagate workspace configuration to it using the same mechanisms as the main tracking deployment. Gateway API HTTPRoute resources are created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for op