bility\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute.\n\n**BRANCH-A-E006 body:**\n\n# Gateway-level artifact traffic routing\n\n## Description\nImplements routing of artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance purely through Gateway API HTTPRoute configuration — no tracking-server configuration change. Selected when the routing design investigation concludes gateway-level routing suffices.\n\nWait — \"Selected when the routing design investigation concludes...\" — that's fine, describes the gating investigation without ID.\n\n## Scope\n- HTTPRoute rules (or equivalent Gateway API configuration) that direct artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance's Service while all other traffic reaches the main tracking deployment.\n- Updates to the main tracking Deployment's rendered configuration needed to point artifact operations at the dedicated instance, within the operator's Helm values conversion.\n- Operator templates and reconciliation for the routing configuration, following the conditional CRD discovery pattern.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Artifact requests that previously reached the main tracking deployment's artifact endpoints are routed to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, verified end-to-end in a cluster with Gateway API CRDs present.\n- Non-artifact traffic (tracking, model registry, prompt registry) continues to reach the main tracking deployment.\n- Workspace validation on the artifacts-only instance is enforced for routed artifact requests (denials return HTTP 403).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\n**BRANCH-B-E006 body:**\n\n# Artifact traffic routing with tracking-server delegation wiring\n\n## Description\nImplements routing of artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance for the outcome where the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. Includes the operator-side wiring that propagates the delegation configuration to the main tracking deployment.\n\n## Scope\n- Operator configuration (Helm values and templates) that configures the main tracking deployment to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance, per the selected routing design.\n- HTTPRoute resources consistent with the delegation-based design.\n- Reconciliation and validation for the delegation configuration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The main tracking deployment's rendered configuration delegates artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected design.\n- Artifact requests reaching the main tracking deployment's artifact endpoints are served via the dedicated instance, verified end-to-end.\n- Non-artifact traffic continues to reach the main tracking deployment.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\n**BRANCH-B-E007 body:**\n\n# Tracking-server artifact delegation configuration\n\n## Description\nImplements the tracking-server side of artifact traffic delegation for the outcome where gateway-level routing alone cannot split artifact traffic: the configuration surface in the MLflow tracking server that delegates artifact operations to the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\n## Scope\n- The tracking-server configuration (flags/settings per the selected routing design) that directs artifact operations to a dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Behavior when the delegation target is unreachable or unconfigured (fail-safe: no silent bypass of the artifacts-only path).\n- Tests covering artifact operations delegated through the configuration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- With the delegation configuration set, artifact operations from the tracking server are served by the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n- Without the delegation configuration, the tracking server's artifact serving behaves exactly as today (backwards compatible).\n- Misconfigured delegation (unreachable target) produces a clear error rather than silently serving artifacts from the tracking server.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance (server-side delegation).\n\nHmm — one concern: BRANCH-B-E007 changes the tracking server, and per NFR \"No existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning\" — a new config is additive. Fine.\n\nNow — wait, I should double-check the \"no cross-references to sibling epics\" rule for branch epics referencing the \"dedicated artifacts-only instance deployed by the operator\" — that's a plain-terms description, no epic ID. Good.\n\nAlso, in E003's body I mention \"the workspace resolution capability verified for the artifacts-only context\" — plain terms. Good.\n\nNow frontmatter commands. Note the epic-task schema requires dependencies as list; the set command example uses `dependencies=\"{ID}-E002,{ID}-E003\"` — the script presumably parses comma-separated into a list. Let me check frontmatter.py's set handling for list fields quickly... The example in the prompt shows comma-separated string for dependencies, so the script handles it. Let me quickly check to be safe.\n\nAlso check how \"set\" writes files that don't have frontmatter yet — the prompt says \"Write the body content (no frontmatter delimiters)\" then set frontmatter via script. So `frontmatter.py set` will add frontmatter to a body-only file. Good.\n\nLet me peek at frontmatter.py briefly for the set semantics (dotted keys, list parsing).","signature":"96fa7290b4834fe4a0248a9e4eacfe43"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":70046,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id"