{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-31616301-bb1f-47ce-b038-d3edb8412226","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86502830e4f011ad","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-8rtkmk24/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\nStrategy status In Progress, priority Critical, effort M-L (4-7 sprints). Three active components (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins), all owned by the MLflow Team. Not below threshold (three architecturally distinct sub-systems across three repos, 4-7 sprints of work) and not docs-only, so the full decomposition flow (Steps 1-8) applies.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Traceability\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. All three corrections are already reflected in the strategy body as fetched:\n\n| Staff Engineer Input | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| No new flag — combine existing `--artifacts-only` + `--enable-workspaces`; remove `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` / `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` | Already incorporated — the strategy's technical approach and HLR-1 describe flag composition only; no references to a new flag or CR field remain in the decomposed scope. |\n| Operator work is a split deployment (dedicated Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute + traffic routing), not a config toggle | Already incorporated — drives the operator epics below (E005 plus the conditional routing branches). |\n| SSAR detail is over-specified — state that authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure; kube-rbac-proxy / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` unchanged | Already incorporated — no epic prescribes Kubernetes API mechanics (SSAR or otherwise); authorization is delegated to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. |\n\nNo divergence between the Staff Engineer Input and the AI-generated technical approach remained to override.\n\n## Component Mapping (canonical Jira components)\n\nEvery epic's `component` is copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The strategy names three components; only one canonical name matches:\n\n| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| MLflow | `MLflow` | Direct match. |\n| MLflow Operator | `MLflow` | No canonical \"MLflow Operator\" entry; the operator is part of the MLflow component's Jira surface. All operator epics use `MLflow`. |\n| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | `MLflow` | No canonical entry; the plugin is the MLflow server's auth/workspace extension. Plugin epics use `MLflow`. |\n\nEpics therefore distinguish operator / server / plugin work by scope and repo, not by Jira component. The single-team, single-canonical-component shape of this strategy does not collapse the epic boundaries: the strategy's Affected Components table lists three distinct code bases (Python server, Go operator, separately packaged Python plugin), which is the boundary that matters for PR review cycles (Epic Boundary Rule 1).\n\nNew-component detection: MLflow and MLflow Operator have architecture-context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/mlflow.md`, `mlflow-operator.md`) — in platform. `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` has **no** architecture-context file but is **not** treated as a new component: the strategy's dependency table marks it \"internal, exists\", and `mlflow.md` corroborates that the plugin (v1.5.0) is already consumed inside the MLflow image via the hermetic Konflux build. No onboarding chain is generated; see Health Warnings.\n\n## Epic List\n\nMain plan (5 epics):\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Validate workspace resolution in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Design artifact traffic routing for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\nConditional branch epics (3, attached to the routing-design Investigation E002; not standalone Jira issues):\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E006 | Gateway-level artifact traffic routing | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 | Artifact traffic routing with tracking-server delegation wiring | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking-server artifact delegation configuration | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n\n`epic_count` counts main-plan epics (5); including branches there are 8 epic files.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigate workspace resolution\"] --> E004[\"E004 Plugin resolution for artifacts-only\"]\n    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Server: workspace-aware artifacts-only\"]\n    E004 --> E003\n    E002[\"E002 Investigate routing design\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator: dedicated artifacts-only instance\"]\n    E002 --> BA[\"BRANCH-A-E006 Gateway-level routing\"]\n    E005 --> BA\n    E002 --> BB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 Routing + delegation wiring\"]\n    E005 --> BB\n    E002 --> BE[\"BRANCH-B-E007 Server delegation config\"]\n```\n\nMain-plan critical path: E001 → E004 → E003 (length 3). Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin epic's scope (verification only vs. refactoring workspace resolution into an independent init path) is determined by the investigation's outcome — a true gate (`rewrite`). |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | The server's workspace-validation approach depends on whether resolution works without tracking stores — true gate (`rewrite`); the flag-composition surface itself is unaffected either way. |\n| E004 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (library → consumer) | The server's artifact-request validation calls the plugin's workspace resolution; the server integration builds against the verified/extended resolution capability. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | The routing design \"affects HTTPRoute design\" (strategy open question) — the artifacts-only instance's ingress shape is a true gate (`rewrite`) on the operator deployment epic; Deployment/Service scope is unaffected. |\n| E002 → BRANCH-A/B-E006, E002 → BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3 | Bounded outcomes (≤3): whether the tracking server needs a configuration change decides which routing branch exists. Conditional branches, `gated_by=E002`, impact `remove` (the other branch replaces this one). |\n| E005 → BRANCH-A-E006, E005 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 13 | The routing configuration builds against the dedicated instance's Service/ingress surface created by the operator deployment work; routing correctness is defined by that target. |\n| (no edge) E003 ↔ E005 / operator epics | Rule 14 | Operator templates in a different repo reference flags, not server code; the operator does not build against server artifacts. The coupling (\"the deployed artifacts-only instance actually runs with the combined flags\") is an AC gate on E005, never a DAG edge. |\n| (no edge) E001-chain ↔ E002-chain | Rule 14 | Server/plugin workspace-resolution work and operator topology work are independent work streams in different repos; they run in parallel and meet at integration testing (ACs). |\n| (no docs-authoring epic) | — | Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes) and the strategy contains no substantive content-authoring scope; no `docs-authoring` epic is generated, so Rule 11 does not apply. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1 — `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-2 — artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (enforcement), E004 (resolution it builds on) |\n| HLR-3 — fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-4 — single-tenant / non-workspace deployments unchanged | P0 | E003 |\n| HLR-5 — operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n| HLR-6 — main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | BRANCH-A-E006, or BRANCH-B-E006 + BRANCH-B-E007 (conditional on E002's outcome — HLR-6 is fully covered in either branch) |\n| HLR-7 — auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context | P1 | E004 |\n| HLR-8 — audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) — instruments the same workspace-denial call sites as HLR-2/HLR-3; a separate epic would reopen and instrument the same validation code paths after the fact, so it is not separately schedulable. |\n\nAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-8's bundling rationale is stated at the row (above) and in E003's HLR Traceability section.\n\n## Open Question and Unknown Disposition\n\nSystematic scan of the Open Questions table, Risks, and Assumptions:\n\n| Unknown (source) | Disposition | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability? (OQ3; also Risk \"auth plugin coupling\"; Assumptions A2, A5) | **Investigation E001** | The answer changes what the plugin and server epics do (verify vs. refactor). Merged with the related assumptions (workspace store queryable without tracking stores; artifact paths carry enough information to resolve membership) — one assessment, one deliverable. |\n| How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from main tracking to the dedicated instance? (OQ2) | **Investigation E002 + conditional branches** | The answer determines whether tracking-server configuration work exists (which epics exist) and the HTTPRoute design (what the operator epics do). Bounded outcomes (gateway-only vs. server-side delegation) → branch epics. |\n| Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request? (OQ1) | **AC + risk note on E003** — not an Investigation | The workspace-validation epic exists and its core work is identical either way; caching is the strategy's own prescribed fix-and-retry mitigation, added only if the measured overhead exceeds the agreed threshold. The epic measures latency and records the caching decision (see Health Warnings — no target specified). |\n| Audit telemetry channel for workspace access denials? (OQ4) | **Scope item + AC on E003** — not an Investigation | HLR-8 (the requirement of record) already fixes the channel to server logs; the PM/Security confirmation is captured in E003's scope with server logs as the default. Resolution is a product confirmation, not technical work that changes which epics exist. |\n| Flags composable without breaking individual-flag init (Assumption A1) | **In-epic validation, E003** | This is the epic's own deliverable, not an upstream unknown; failure would be a redesign inside the same epic. |\n| Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes (Assumption A4) | **In-epic validation, E005** | Implementation detail resolved when the team starts; structural adjustments, if needed, are within the same epic. |\n| Single-tenant deployments unaffected (Assumption A3) | **AC on E003** | Backwards-compatibility regression criterion. |\n\nPrerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, License Validation, etc.) are all \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level — they are process gates upstream of every epic, recorded as a health warning, not modeled as epics.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and exist (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy per the strategy's dependency table); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed. The two work streams (server/plugin chain and operator chain) already run in parallel.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins lacks an architecture-context file** (context-coverage gap). Treated as an existing internal component per the strategy's dependency table (\"internal, exists\") and `mlflow.md` (plugin v1.5.0 already consumed in the MLflow image). If plugin changes turn out to require upstream/PyPI release coordination beyond the MLflow Team, E004's external-dependency handling should be revisited (and its `external_dependency` signal re-scored).\n2. **No latency target specified** (OQ1, owner PM/Engineering; NFR Performance explicitly defers to Open Questions). Caching at launch vs. follow-up is unresolved. Captured as a measurable AC on E003 (documented latency figure + recorded caching decision) — a scope trap to close before launch planning.\n3. **HLR-8 vs. OQ4 inconsistency**: HLR-8 mandates audit logging \"through server logs\" while OQ4 leaves the telemetry channel (OTLP / Kubernetes audit log / server log) to PM/Security. E003 defaults to server logs per the HLR and records the channel confirmation as a scope item; if a different channel is mandated, only the emission mechanism inside E003 changes.\n4. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, License Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\". These are human approval steps upstream of all epics (a strategy-level, not epic-level, gate) and are noted here for planning awareness.\n5. **Priority inheritance, not inversion**: E004 (plugin) is P0 because it enables the P0 server epic E003, although its own HLR-7 is P1. If E001 finds no initialization coupling, E004 collapses to quick verification and the P0 label is harmless; if coupling is found, the P0 label correctly pulls the refactor forward.\n6. **Epic sizing**: the strategy's 2-3 sprint operator estimate is split along the HLR-5 / HLR-6 boundary (E005 ≈ dedicated instance deployment; routing in the branch epics) so each operator epic sits near the ~2-week guideline. E003 carries the strategy's 1-2 sprint server estimate plus the bundled P2 audit logging — within the guideline; if the audit-channel decision or caching requirement lands as substantial extra work, that is the trigger to re-split E003 (measurable condition: E003's estimate exceeds 2 sprints once the latency threshold and audit channel decisions are recorded).\n7. **Fail-closed AC detail**: AC 4 in the strategy specifies HTTP 503 for workspace-store-unreachable denials while HLR-3 says only \"requests are denied\" — E003 implements 503 per the more specific AC; the 403-vs-503 split (membership failure vs. validation unavailable) is intentional.\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\nThe routing-design Investigation (E002) has two bounded outcomes; the branch epics below are attached to it as a plan document, not created as standalone Jira issues:\n\n- **Branch A — gateway-level routing suffices** (no tracking-server change): `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E006.md` (operator: HTTPRoute-based routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated instance).\n- **Branch B — tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations**: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006.md` (operator: routing plus delegation wiring propagated to the main tracking deployment) and `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007.md` (MLflow server: the delegation configuration itself).\n\nAll branch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=E002` (also in `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact` (`remove` — the other branch's epics replace this one if the outcome differs). No main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; HLR-6 is covered entirely within whichever branch is selected.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":78566,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a59a5dd3308e877f","agent_id":"ab656e2c2f20f84c9","session_id":"b33f4375-e5e7-42e1-a4f6-de864124642b","uuid":"a84271d9-bd41-46b8-a2bb-da9174584350","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:35:59.297Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
