{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2bdd7ba0437a82f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n9\t  description: E003 AC 7 (latency) is satisfied only against \"the PM/Engineering-agreed\n10\t    threshold\" (strategy OQ1) — an input controlled by parties outside the epic. Reword\n11\t    the AC to be self-contained (document the measured figure and the caching decision,\n12\t    recording the absence of an agreed threshold if none exists) or move the threshold-agreement\n13\t    dependency to Risks/Notes.\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 5-epic main plan plus 3 conditional branch epics is the expected shape. Graded below is only what the validator cannot check.\n22\t\n23\tVerified in detail: all 8 HLRs map to epics (HLR-8's P2 bundling into E003 is recorded with rationale in both the matrix row and E003's HLR Traceability section, and shares the implementation surface with HLR-2/3 — compliant, not priority collapse); every DAG-diagram edge matches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly in both directions, with arrows drawn dependency → dependent; no circular dependencies; no main-plan epic depends on a branch epic; all three branch files set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002` with `gate_failure_impact` populated and the gate present in direct `dependencies`; both Investigations genuinely change downstream structure (verify-vs-refactor for E001; branch selection for E002) and every Investigation dependency carries `gated_by`; priority inheritance holds (E001/E004 are P0 blocking P0 work; E002 gates only P1 work); signal values in frontmatter match the ai-signals rationale files for all 8 epics and calibrate against both rubrics (including `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` for E001/E002, which matches the rubric's \"peripheral questions need a live cluster\" condition, and `architecture_claims: +1` with named context-file citations on every Implementation epic); the E003↔E005 no-edge shape is the rule-compliant configuration-reference coupling (flags rendered into the Deployment command, gated by E005's AC on the exact container command), not a missing artifact dependency; no epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; no scope duplication across siblings (E003's artifacts-only flag composition and BRANCH-B-E007's tracking-server delegation are distinct configuration surfaces in the same repo); no work-product type mixing; Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes), so the absence of a docs-authoring epic is correct.\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\tAll four P0 HLRs (1-4) map to E003 (with E004 supporting HLR-2); P1 HLRs 5-7 map to E005, the conditional routing branches (HLR-6 covered in full by either branch — BRANCH-A-E006 alone, or BRANCH-B-E006 + BRANCH-B-E007), and E004; P2 HLR-8 is bundled into E003 with the bundling rationale recorded in the matrix row and repeated in E003's HLR Traceability section, and it instruments the same denial call sites as HLR-2/3 — not separately schedulable, so not priority collapse. Priority inheritance is sound: E001 (P0) and E004 (P0) block P0 work; E002 (P1) gates only P1 work (HLR-5/6). Health Warning 5 correctly documents E004's P0 label as inheritance from the P0 server epic it enables.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n31\tDiagram and frontmatter are mutually consistent edge-for-edge (E001→E003, E001→E004, E004→E003, E002→E005, E002→{BA-E006, BB-E006, BB-E007}, E005→{BA-E006, BB-E006}); arrows run dependency → dependent per the `graph TD` convention. No circular dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics, no main-plan dependency on branch nodes. Each edge is justified in the summary's DAG Justification table under Rules 3/12/13. The absence of E003↔E005 coupling is correctly handled as a Rule 14 configuration reference: the operator renders flags into the Deployment command (E005 AC 2 gates the exact combined command line), which is an AC gate, not a DAG edge — reporting it as a missing edge would be wrong. The two chains (server/plugin and operator/routing) are genuinely parallel work in different repos meeting at integration ACs. Critical path 3 (E001→E004→E003) is reasonable for an M-L strategy whose size is breadth (two parallel streams), not depth. Branch files all satisfy the branch contract (`branch`, `gated_by` in direct `dependencies`, `gate_failure_impact: remove` with the cross-branch replacement named).\n32\t\n33\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n34\tAll epics use the canonical component `MLflow` (verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt` — no \"MLflow Operator\" or \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" entries exist) and team `MLflow Team`; boundaries are drawn along the three codebases (Python server, Go operator, separately packaged plugin), which the summary explicitly justifies as the boundary that matters for PR review cycles. No epic spans codebases: E003 (server), E004 (plugin), E005/BA-E006/BB-E006 (operator), BB-E007 (server). No scope duplication: E003 and BRANCH-B-E007 touch different configuration surfaces (combined flags on the artifacts-only instance vs delegation config on the main tracking server), and the mutually exclusive branch epics are not siblings in the same plan. E003's size (strategy's 1-2 sprint server estimate plus bundled P2 audit logging) carries a Health Warnings entry with estimate, why-not-split rationale, and a concrete re-split trigger — the defensible planning trade-off shape, not docked. No work-product type mixing.\n35\t\n36\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n37\tBoth Investigations resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure: E001's outcome determines verify-vs-refactor for E004 and the validation approach for E003; E002's outcome selects which routing branch exists. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with `gated_by` has `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated; every epic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set; every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list (E001 for E003/E004; E002 for E005 and all three branch epics).\n38\t\n39\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n40\tType-appropriate signal fields are used everywhere: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals`, all six Implementation epics carry `ai_signals`, and each of the 8 epics has its `-ai-signals.md` rationale file with values matching frontmatter. Investigation signals calibrate against the 5-signal rubric: `question_specificity: +1` (enumerable questions with pass/fail verdicts), `source_accessibility: +1` (answers determinable from plugin/server/operator sources), `local_runnability: 0` (the `kubernetes://` workspace store and Gateway API HTTPRoutes genuinely need a cluster), `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` (peripheral runtime confirmation needs a live cluster; gating questions are source-answerable — exactly the rubric's -1 condition), `human_judgment_required: 0` (both questions are team-resolved technical decisions, not stakeholder gates). Implementation signals are justified: E004's cautious `change_specificity: 0` / `open_questions: -1` / `external_dependency: 0` / `repo_access: 0` honestly reflect the verify-vs-refactor uncertainty, the PyPI release-coordination question, and the unidentified plugin repo; `architecture_claims: +1` is scored with named context-file citations (`mlflow.md`, `mlflow-operator.md`) on every Implementation epic, satisfying the calibration check — the strategy's premises about the server flags, plugin, and operator patterns are corroborated by the context files. No arithmetic or threshold checks performed (pipeline-computed).\n41\t\n42\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n43\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's Given/When/Then criteria (E003 mirrors strategy ACs 1-4 plus the fail-to-start and audit cases; E005 mirrors ACs 5-6 plus misconfiguration rejection and conditional HTTPRoute creation; branch epics carry end-to-end routing and 403 verification). Rule-mandated ACs: no replacement, docs-authoring, or konflux-chain epics exist, so none apply; backwards-compatibility ACs are present on E003, E005, and BRANCH-B-E007 regardless. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID. One minor soft-AC finding (below): E003's latency AC leans on a PM/Engineering-owned threshold. \"Verified end-to-end in a cluster with Gateway API CRDs present\" (BRANCH-A-E006) is a valid AC — the test environment is engineer-provisionable, not a party-granted availability.\n44\t\n45\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n46\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: flag composition, workspace validation with 403, fail-closed 503 (with the 403/503 split explained in Health Warning 7), single-tenant regression, operator split deployment with kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and misconfiguration validation, artifact traffic routing, plugin resolution, and audit logging all map to epic scope/ACs. All five assumptions are dispositioned (A1/E003, A2+A5/E001, A3/E003 AC, A4/E005), all four open questions are dispositioned (OQ3→E001, OQ2→E002+branches, OQ1→E003 AC+risk note, OQ4→E003 scope item with server-log default), and all four risks are carried into epic scope. The strategy's integration-testing line item is distributed as end-to-end ACs (E003 regression coverage, branch routing verification) rather than a standalone epic — deliberate and on the record in the summary. Conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes of the routing question, with HLR-6 fully covered in either. Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not Yes, so no docs-authoring epic is required (Rule 11 not triggered), and the summary records this explicitly.\n47\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6fda20157b7eec7","agent_id":"a3f697b0c5a7ec8e0","session_id":"b33f4375-e5e7-42e1-a4f6-de864124642b","uuid":"9087ce05-e27c-44ae-83d9-43b4bf4ac432","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:43:52.392Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2bdd7ba0437a82f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
