{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b08e43414d6deed9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If workspace resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization,\n16\t    the validation approach is rewritten to build on the refactored independent initialization\n17\t    path; the flag-composition surface is unchanged.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: 0\n24\t  external_dependency: 1\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 7\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tMakes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and leaves tracking stores and model registry stores uninitialized with non-artifact API endpoints disabled. Each artifact request is resolved to its parent experiment, its workspace assignment looked up via the workspace store, and authorization delegated to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403. Behavior is fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable, and workspace access denials are audit-logged. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining the two existing flags.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Combine `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if missing when `--enable-workspaces` is set) and initialize only the workspace provider needed for artifact request validation, per the validated assessment of the plugin's initialization dependencies.\n44\t- Keep tracking stores, model registry stores, and non-artifact API endpoints uninitialized/disabled in the combined mode; validate that the combined flags do not break the server initialization sequence for either flag used individually.\n45\t- Add workspace validation to artifact request handling: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (S3 artifact store layout `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; deny with HTTP 403 and an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions on membership failure. Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n46\t- Implement fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny artifact requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable rather than serving without validation.\n47\t- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership); no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n48\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs — the channel fixed by the strategy's P2 requirement. Confirm the telemetry channel with PM/Security (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log, or server log) before implementation and adjust the emission mechanism if a channel other than server logs is mandated; the denial events and their content are the same either way.\n49\t- Measure per-request workspace resolution latency and record the measured figure together with the caching decision — implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions (consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path) or record caching as a follow-up. The decision is evaluated against the PM/Engineering-agreed threshold when one exists; if no threshold has been agreed at completion, the absence is recorded explicitly and the decision is made and justified against the measured figure alone (see Risks / Notes).\n50\t- Regression coverage: single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Given the artifacts-only server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n55\t- Given the same deployment, a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n56\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n57\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user requesting an artifact receives it without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership).\n58\t- Given `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` set without `--workspace-store-uri`, the server fails to start with a clear error.\n59\t- Workspace access denials produce audit records through server logs (or the PM/Security-selected channel) containing enough context to identify the denied request.\n60\t- A measured latency figure for per-request workspace resolution is documented (in the epic's test results or design notes), together with the caching decision — short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions implemented in this epic, or caching recorded as a follow-up. The decision is stated against the PM/Engineering-agreed threshold when one exists; if no threshold has been agreed at completion, that absence is recorded explicitly and the decision is justified against the measured figure alone.\n61\t\n62\t## HLR Traceability\n63\t\n64\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving — covered.\n65\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403 — covered.\n66\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable — covered.\n67\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior — covered.\n68\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials is bundled into this epic rather than split out — it instruments the same workspace-denial call sites as HLR-2 and HLR-3, so a separate epic would reopen and instrument the same validation code paths after the fact; it is not separately schedulable. Bundling is a scope/AC item here, not a deferral.\n69\t\n70\t## Risks / Notes\n71\t\n72\t- The latency threshold for the caching decision is an input owned outside this epic: strategy open question OQ1 (owner PM/Engineering) — no target is specified in the RFE or the architecture context. The epic's latency AC is therefore self-contained (documented measured figure plus recorded caching decision, with the absence of an agreed threshold recorded if none exists) and does not depend on the threshold being agreed for the epic to complete. Securing the threshold agreement is a strategy-level open item to close before launch planning, not work in this epic.\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aedc5e6f7f2746b2","agent_id":"adfa70934f3d680a7","session_id":"b33f4375-e5e7-42e1-a4f6-de864124642b","uuid":"0b7130e6-478b-4408-8b17-24e305efd291","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:45:08.408Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b08e43414d6deed9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
